            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for a memorial, then the Latin for offerings to the dead."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: hērēs, hērēdis",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "hērēs, hērēdis — \"an heir\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Fīlius ūnus hērēs patris erat. The only son was his father's heir."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Masculine or feminine, 3rd declension. Hērēdem facere: to name someone heir in a will. English heir."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "hērēs is inherited; EtymDB traces it to a Proto-Indo-European root meaning \"to leave behind\", while Lewis & Short compare a Sanskrit root meaning \"to take\", so the details are not settled. English heir comes from it through French."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
